1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,928
The client: one CLI, two channels.

2
00:00:02,928 --> 00:00:06,169
Balance commands go to the account service,

3
00:00:06,169 --> 00:00:09,873
transfers and history go to the transaction service.

4
00:00:09,873 --> 00:00:14,503
Then the test suite that keeps all of this honest.

5
00:00:14,803 --> 00:00:21,547
The client is a thin CLI with two channels, because it talks to two services.

6
00:00:21,547 --> 00:00:25,144
Balance commands go straight to the account service,

7
00:00:25,144 --> 00:00:28,741
transfers and history go to the transaction service.

8
00:00:28,741 --> 00:00:33,238
That mirrors how a real client would address separate backends.

9
00:00:33,538 --> 00:00:35,948
Every call passes a deadline.

10
00:00:35,948 --> 00:00:37,148
Without it,

11
00:00:37,148 --> 00:00:44,861
a hung server blocks the client thread forever and the user just sees a frozen terminal.

12
00:00:44,926 --> 00:00:49,443
Errors print the status code and the server's detail string.

13
00:00:49,443 --> 00:00:56,219
The code is what a program branches on, the detail is what a human reads.

14
00:00:56,219 --> 00:00:59,382
In the demo you will see FAILED_PRECONDITION,

15
00:00:59,382 --> 00:01:02,544
NOT_FOUND and INVALID_ARGUMENT telling three different stories.

16
00:01:02,844 --> 00:01:08,379
History renders direction as a sign: out is minus, in is plus,

17
00:01:08,379 --> 00:01:11,608
and balance_after sits next to the amount.

18
00:01:11,608 --> 00:01:17,605
So the table reads like a bank statement instead of a debug dump.

19
00:01:17,905 --> 00:01:23,737
The demo command is a scripted version of the whole run: happy path,

20
00:01:23,737 --> 00:01:25,532
then every error case,

21
00:01:25,532 --> 00:01:30,467
then re-read the balances to prove the rejected calls changed nothing.

22
00:01:30,467 --> 00:01:36,748
I scripted it so the recording is reproducible and I am not typing live.

23
00:01:37,048 --> 00:01:42,711
Ten tests, and they spin both services up in process on ephemeral ports,

24
00:01:42,711 --> 00:01:46,195
so they do not need the servers running.

25
00:01:46,195 --> 00:01:51,858
They cover the seeded balance, a signed delta, a transfer conserving total money,

26
00:01:51,858 --> 00:01:54,907
insufficient funds leaving balances untouched, unknown account,

27
00:01:54,907 --> 00:01:59,263
four invalid argument cases, history ordering and limit, empty history,

28
00:01:59,263 --> 00:02:04,054
a failed credit rolling back the debit, and fifty concurrent updates.

29
00:02:04,354 --> 00:02:12,325
Green across the board, and the rollback test is the one I would point a reviewer at,

30
00:02:12,325 --> 00:02:17,483
because it exercises the failure path rather than the happy one.

